export const HELP_TOPICS = [
 {
  "id": "getting-started",
  "title": "Getting started",
  "summary": "Install the program, sign in for the first time, and get ready to print your first card.",
  "html": "<p>Install the program, sign in for the first time, and get ready to print your first card.</p>\n<h3 id=\"install\">Install</h3>\n<ol><li>Download the setup from the program's GitHub releases page and run it.</li><li>If Windows shows <i>Windows protected your PC</i>, choose <b>More info</b>, then <b>Run anyway</b>. This only happens the first time, because the setup isn't code-signed.</li><li>Follow the setup: read the licence and choose <b>I accept the agreement</b>. It installs the Microsoft components the program needs if they're missing.</li><li>Tick <b>Start automatically when anyone signs in to Windows</b> if you want printing to be ready as soon as the PC starts.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"sign-in-for-the-first-time\">Sign in for the first time</h3>\n<ol><li>Open <b>ID Card Printing Software Free</b> from the Start menu.</li><li>Sign in with the username <code>admin</code> and the password <code>admin</code>.</li><li>Choose your own password (at least 8 characters). You can't use the program until you do.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"your-first-card-step-by-step\">Your first card, step by step</h3>\n<ol><li>Choose your card printer in <b>Settings</b>. See <a href=\"#/help/printer-setup-and-status\">Printer setup and status</a>.</li><li>Create a card template in <b>Card templates</b>. See <a href=\"#/help/card-templates\">Card templates</a>.</li><li>Add a client in <b>Clients</b>, such as <i>Example Company</i>. See <a href=\"#/help/clients\">Clients</a>.</li><li>Add the client's people one at a time, or <a href=\"#/help/importing-people-from-a-spreadsheet\">import them from a spreadsheet</a>.</li><li>Tick the people and choose <b>Print</b>. See <a href=\"#/help/printing-cards\">Printing cards</a>.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"finding-your-way-around\">Finding your way around</h3>\n<p>The sidebar on the left takes you to each part of the program:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Sidebar</th><th>What it's for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Clients</td><td>The organisations you print for, their people and their cards</td></tr><tr><td>Card templates</td><td>Designing the cards (administrators only)</td></tr><tr><td>Print queue</td><td>Cards waiting to print, printing now and just printed</td></tr><tr><td>Print history</td><td>Every card ever printed</td></tr><tr><td>Settings</td><td>Printer, printing options, users, backups and appearance</td></tr><tr><td>Help</td><td>This guide</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Below the menu, the printer status shows whether the printer is ready. Click it for the printer's details.</p>\n<p>Press <b>F1</b> at any time to open Help, and type in its search box to find a topic.</p>\n<div class=\"note\"><b>Tip:</b> Closing the window doesn't stop the program. It keeps running in the notification area by the clock, so cards keep printing. See <a href=\"#/help/closing-the-program\">Closing the program</a>.</div>",
  "text": "Install the program, sign in for the first time, and get ready to print your first card. Install Download the setup from the program's GitHub releases page and run it. If Windows shows Windows protected your PC, choose More info, then Run anyway. This only happens the first time, because the setup isn't code-signed. Follow the setup: read the licence and choose I accept the agreement. It installs the Microsoft components the program needs if they're missing. Tick Start automatically when anyone signs in to Windows if you want printing to be ready as soon as the PC starts. Sign in for the first time Open ID Card Printing Software Free from the Start menu. Sign in with the username admin and the password admin. Choose your own password (at least 8 characters). You can't use the program until you do. Your first card, step by step Choose your card printer in Settings. See Printer setup and status. Create a card template in Card templates. See Card templates. Add a client in Clients, such as Example Company. See Clients. Add the client's people one at a time, or import them from a spreadsheet. Tick the people and choose Print. See Printing cards. Finding your way around The sidebar on the left takes you to each part of the program: Sidebar What it's for Clients The organisations you print for, their people and their cards Card templates Designing the cards (administrators only) Print queue Cards waiting to print, printing now and just printed Print history Every card ever printed Settings Printer, printing options, users, backups and appearance Help This guide Below the menu, the printer status shows whether the printer is ready. Click it for the printer's details. Press F1 at any time to open Help, and type in its search box to find a topic. Tip: Closing the window doesn't stop the program. It keeps running in the notification area by the clock, so cards keep printing. See Closing the program."
 },
 {
  "id": "card-templates",
  "title": "Card templates",
  "summary": "A card template is the design of a card: its size, background, the photo frame, and where each person's details go. Administrators design templates in Card templates.",
  "html": "<p>A card template is the design of a card: its size, background, the photo frame, and where each person's details go. Administrators design templates in <b>Card templates</b>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"create-a-template\">Create a template</h3>\n<ol><li>Choose <b>Card templates</b> in the sidebar, then <b>New template</b>.</li><li>Type a name, such as <i>Staff card</i>.</li><li>Under <b>Start from</b>, choose <b>A blank card</b>, or a copy of an existing template.</li><li>Choose <b>Create and open</b>. The template builder opens.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"choose-the-card-size\">Choose the card size</h3>\n<p>In the builder's left panel, under <b>Card size</b>, choose:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Size</th><th>Card (mm)</th><th>Pixels at 300 dpi</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CR80</td><td>85.6 × 54</td><td>1013 × 638</td><td>The usual ID card</td></tr><tr><td>CR79</td><td>83.9 × 52.1</td><td>991 × 615</td><td>Adhesive-backed cards</td></tr><tr><td>CR100</td><td>98.5 × 67</td><td>1163 × 791</td><td>Larger ID cards and badges</td></tr><tr><td>Custom</td><td>20 to 150</td><td>Worked out from the size</td><td>Any other size</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Then choose <b>Portrait</b> or <b>Landscape</b> under <b>Card orientation</b>. Your printer must accept the card size you choose.</p>\n<h3 id=\"add-a-background\">Add a background</h3>\n<ol><li>Under <b>Front background</b>, choose <b>Choose image</b> and pick a picture: JPG, PNG, HEIC (iPhone photos), WebP, TIFF, AVIF, BMP, GIF or SVG.</li><li>Or pick a <b>Background colour</b> instead.</li><li>Choose <b>Save template</b> to keep it.</li></ol>\n<p>The picture fills the whole card. If it's a different shape from the card, its edges are trimmed rather than squashed, so for the best result make it the card's shape, for example 638 × 1013 pixels for a portrait CR80 card.</p>\n<p>To fade the background picture, for example behind text, drag <b>Image opacity</b> below it. To make it darker or richer, drag <b>Darkness</b> (which darkens the whole picture, white areas too), <b>Contrast</b> and <b>Colour</b>. Logos and the ID photo frame have the same three settings when selected; on the photo frame they apply to every person's photo.</p>\n<h3 id=\"put-peoples-details-on-the-card\">Put people's details on the card</h3>\n<ol><li>Under <b>Fields</b>, tick each detail the card shows: <b>Photo</b>, <b>First name</b>, <b>Last name</b>, <b>Staff ID number</b>, <b>Position</b>, <b>Title</b>, <b>Address</b>, and any <a href=\"#/help/person-fields\">person fields</a> you've added.</li><li>Drag each ticked field's <b>Drag onto card</b> button onto the card, or click it to place it in the middle.</li><li>Drag a field to move it, and drag its edges to resize it. Guide lines appear when it lines up with the card or other fields.</li></ol>\n<p>The <b>Sample text</b> switch above the card shows example details (<i>Alex Sample</i>) so you can see how the card will look.</p>\n<h3 id=\"add-text-logos-shapes-and-barcodes\">Add text, logos, shapes and barcodes</h3>\n<p>Under <b>Design</b>, add things that are the same on every card:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Add text</b> for fixed words, such as <i>VISITOR</i>.</li><li><b>Add logo or image</b> for a logo. Choose <b>Fit inside</b> to show the whole image, or <b>Fill box</b> to fill the box and crop the edges.</li><li><b>Add shape</b> for coloured bands and boxes, with a fill, border and rounded corners.</li><li><b>Add barcode or QR code</b>: choose the <b>Type</b> (QR code, Data Matrix, PDF417, Code 128 or Code 39) and the <b>Data</b> it holds, such as the Staff ID number, or fixed text.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"style-the-selected-field\">Style the selected field</h3>\n<p>Click a field on the card to select it. The right panel shows its settings:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Text</b>: font, weight, size, colour, <b>CAPITALS</b>, alignment, rotation and letter spacing. Text always stays inside its box: a long name shrinks down to the <b>Smallest</b> size, then moves onto two lines if the box has room, then shrinks further.</li><li><b>Dates</b>: choose the <b>Date format</b>, such as 31/03/2027 or 31 Mar 2027.</li><li><b>Photo</b>: the frame's photo size shows under <b>Photos</b>, such as <i>At least 360 × 480 px · 30.5 × 40.6 mm · 3:4 portrait</i>. Choose <b>Fill the frame</b> (photos of another shape are trimmed at the edges) or <b>Whole photo</b> (nothing is cut off; any space shows the frame's colour). To line everyone's faces up instead, turn on <b>Line up faces on every card</b> in <b>Settings</b> (see <a href=\"#/help/people\">Line up faces</a>). Also corner radius, border, the frame's colour, and <b>Make 3:4 portrait</b> for the usual photo shape. Each person's photo can still be moved and zoomed when you add or edit them.</li><li><b>Position and size</b>: exact X, Y, width and height in pixels, plus <b>Centre horizontally</b>, <b>Centre vertically</b>, <b>Centre on card</b> and <b>Full width</b>.</li></ul>\n<p>Use the bin button to remove the selected field from the card.</p>\n<h3 id=\"layer-order-and-opacity\">Layer order and opacity</h3>\n<p>Things on the card are stacked: anything higher up is drawn over what's below it. With a field selected, under <b>Arrange</b>:</p>\n<ul><li><b>To front</b> and <b>To back</b> move it to the top or bottom of the stack.</li><li><b>Forward</b> and <b>Backward</b> move it up or down one place.</li><li><b>Opacity</b> fades it: 100% is solid, lower values let what's behind show through, for example a faded logo watermark.</li></ul>\n<p>The <b>On the front</b> list (shown when nothing is selected) lists everything in stacking order, top first. Clicking where things overlap selects the one on top.</p>\n<h3 id=\"keyboard-shortcuts-in-the-builder\">Keyboard shortcuts in the builder</h3>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Keys</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Arrow keys</td><td>Move the selected field 1 pixel</td></tr><tr><td>Shift + arrow keys</td><td>Move it 10 pixels</td></tr><tr><td>Ctrl + ] / Ctrl + [</td><td>Bring the selected field forward / send it backward</td></tr><tr><td>Ctrl + Shift + ] / Ctrl + Shift + [</td><td>Bring it to the front / send it to the back</td></tr><tr><td>Delete</td><td>Remove the selected field</td></tr><tr><td>Esc</td><td>Deselect</td></tr><tr><td>Ctrl + Z</td><td>Undo</td></tr><tr><td>Ctrl + Y</td><td>Redo</td></tr><tr><td>Ctrl + S</td><td>Save the template</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"the-back-of-the-card\">The back of the card</h3>\n<ol><li>Choose <b>Back</b> above the card.</li><li>Turn on <b>Print a back</b>.</li><li>Add a background and fields as you did for the front.</li></ol>\n<p>See <a href=\"#/help/dual-sided-cards\">Dual-sided cards</a> for printing both sides.</p>\n<h3 id=\"save-and-use-the-template\">Save and use the template</h3>\n<ol><li>Choose <b>Save template</b>. The top bar shows <b>Unsaved changes</b> until you do.</li><li>Choose <b>Assign to clients</b> and tick the clients who use it.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Duplicate</b> makes a copy to start a similar design. The bin button at the top deletes the template.</p>",
  "text": "A card template is the design of a card: its size, background, the photo frame, and where each person's details go. Administrators design templates in Card templates. Create a template Choose Card templates in the sidebar, then New template. Type a name, such as Staff card. Under Start from, choose A blank card, or a copy of an existing template. Choose Create and open. The template builder opens. Choose the card size In the builder's left panel, under Card size, choose: Size Card (mm) Pixels at 300 dpi Use CR80 85.6 × 54 1013 × 638 The usual ID card CR79 83.9 × 52.1 991 × 615 Adhesive-backed cards CR100 98.5 × 67 1163 × 791 Larger ID cards and badges Custom 20 to 150 Worked out from the size Any other size Then choose Portrait or Landscape under Card orientation. Your printer must accept the card size you choose. Add a background Under Front background, choose Choose image and pick a picture: JPG, PNG, HEIC (iPhone photos), WebP, TIFF, AVIF, BMP, GIF or SVG. Or pick a Background colour instead. Choose Save template to keep it. The picture fills the whole card. If it's a different shape from the card, its edges are trimmed rather than squashed, so for the best result make it the card's shape, for example 638 × 1013 pixels for a portrait CR80 card. To fade the background picture, for example behind text, drag Image opacity below it. To make it darker or richer, drag Darkness (which darkens the whole picture, white areas too), Contrast and Colour. Logos and the ID photo frame have the same three settings when selected; on the photo frame they apply to every person's photo. Put people's details on the card Under Fields, tick each detail the card shows: Photo, First name, Last name, Staff ID number, Position, Title, Address, and any person fields you've added. Drag each ticked field's Drag onto card button onto the card, or click it to place it in the middle. Drag a field to move it, and drag its edges to resize it. Guide lines appear when it lines up with the card or other fields. The Sample text switch above the card shows example details (Alex Sample) so you can see how the card will look. Add text, logos, shapes and barcodes Under Design, add things that are the same on every card: Add text for fixed words, such as VISITOR. Add logo or image for a logo. Choose Fit inside to show the whole image, or Fill box to fill the box and crop the edges. Add shape for coloured bands and boxes, with a fill, border and rounded corners. Add barcode or QR code: choose the Type (QR code, Data Matrix, PDF417, Code 128 or Code 39) and the Data it holds, such as the Staff ID number, or fixed text. Style the selected field Click a field on the card to select it. The right panel shows its settings: Text: font, weight, size, colour, CAPITALS, alignment, rotation and letter spacing. Text always stays inside its box: a long name shrinks down to the Smallest size, then moves onto two lines if the box has room, then shrinks further. Dates: choose the Date format, such as 31/03/2027 or 31 Mar 2027. Photo: the frame's photo size shows under Photos, such as At least 360 × 480 px · 30.5 × 40.6 mm · 3:4 portrait. Choose Fill the frame (photos of another shape are trimmed at the edges) or Whole photo (nothing is cut off; any space shows the frame's colour). To line everyone's faces up instead, turn on Line up faces on every card in Settings (see Line up faces). Also corner radius, border, the frame's colour, and Make 3:4 portrait for the usual photo shape. Each person's photo can still be moved and zoomed when you add or edit them. Position and size: exact X, Y, width and height in pixels, plus Centre horizontally, Centre vertically, Centre on card and Full width. Use the bin button to remove the selected field from the card. Layer order and opacity Things on the card are stacked: anything higher up is drawn over what's below it. With a field selected, under Arrange: To front and To back move it to the top or bottom of the stack. Forward and Backward move it up or down one place. Opacity fades it: 100% is solid, lower values let what's behind show through, for example a faded logo watermark. The On the front list (shown when nothing is selected) lists everything in stacking order, top first. Clicking where things overlap selects the one on top. Keyboard shortcuts in the builder Keys Does Arrow keys Move the selected field 1 pixel Shift + arrow keys Move it 10 pixels Ctrl + ] / Ctrl + [ Bring the selected field forward / send it backward Ctrl + Shift + ] / Ctrl + Shift + [ Bring it to the front / send it to the back Delete Remove the selected field Esc Deselect Ctrl + Z Undo Ctrl + Y Redo Ctrl + S Save the template The back of the card Choose Back above the card. Turn on Print a back. Add a background and fields as you did for the front. See Dual-sided cards for printing both sides. Save and use the template Choose Save template. The top bar shows Unsaved changes until you do. Choose Assign to clients and tick the clients who use it. Duplicate makes a copy to start a similar design. The bin button at the top deletes the template."
 },
 {
  "id": "clients",
  "title": "Clients",
  "summary": "A client is an organisation you print cards for, such as Example Company. Each client has its own people, card templates and print history.",
  "html": "<p>A client is an organisation you print cards for, such as <i>Example Company</i>. Each client has its own people, card templates and print history.</p>\n<h3 id=\"add-a-client\">Add a client</h3>\n<ol><li>Choose <b>Clients</b> in the sidebar, then <b>New client</b>.</li><li>Type the <b>Client name</b>, and the contact name, phone, email and notes if you want them.</li><li>Under <b>Card template</b>, choose the template the client uses. Leave <b>Make a separate copy for this client</b> ticked so changes to their card don't affect other clients.</li><li>Choose <b>Create client</b>.</li></ol>\n<p>Search for a client with the search box above the list.</p>\n<h3 id=\"a-clients-tabs\">A client's tabs</h3>\n<p>Open a client to see four tabs:</p>\n<ul><li><b>People</b>: everyone who gets a card. See <a href=\"#/help/people\">People</a>.</li><li><b>Card templates</b>: the templates this client uses (administrators only).</li><li><b>Print history</b>: the cards printed for this client. See <a href=\"#/help/print-history\">Print history</a>.</li><li><b>Details</b>: the client's name, contact details and notes. Change them and choose <b>Save details</b>.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"choose-a-clients-templates\">Choose a client's templates</h3>\n<ol><li>Open the client's <b>Card templates</b> tab.</li><li>Choose <b>Choose templates</b> and tick the templates the client uses, or <b>New template</b> to design a new one for them.</li><li>Choose <b>Make default</b> on the template that should be picked when you print.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"delete-a-client\">Delete a client</h3>\n<p>Administrators can delete a client on the <b>Details</b> tab: choose the <b>Delete</b> button with the client's name, type the client's name to confirm, and choose <b>Delete client</b>. This removes the client's people, photos and templates for good, so <a href=\"#/help/backups-and-moving-to-a-new-pc\">make a backup</a> first if you might need them.</p>",
  "text": "A client is an organisation you print cards for, such as Example Company. Each client has its own people, card templates and print history. Add a client Choose Clients in the sidebar, then New client. Type the Client name, and the contact name, phone, email and notes if you want them. Under Card template, choose the template the client uses. Leave Make a separate copy for this client ticked so changes to their card don't affect other clients. Choose Create client. Search for a client with the search box above the list. A client's tabs Open a client to see four tabs: People: everyone who gets a card. See People. Card templates: the templates this client uses (administrators only). Print history: the cards printed for this client. See Print history. Details: the client's name, contact details and notes. Change them and choose Save details. Choose a client's templates Open the client's Card templates tab. Choose Choose templates and tick the templates the client uses, or New template to design a new one for them. Choose Make default on the template that should be picked when you print. Delete a client Administrators can delete a client on the Details tab: choose the Delete button with the client's name, type the client's name to confirm, and choose Delete client. This removes the client's people, photos and templates for good, so make a backup first if you might need them."
 },
 {
  "id": "people",
  "title": "People",
  "summary": "People are the card holders in a client. Open a client to see their People tab.",
  "html": "<p>People are the card holders in a client. Open a client to see their <b>People</b> tab.</p>\n<h3 id=\"add-a-person\">Add a person</h3>\n<ol><li>Open the client and choose <b>Add person</b>.</li><li>Type their first and last name, and any other details the card shows.</li><li>Drop their ID photo onto the photo box, or click it to choose a photo. The box shows the photo size the template needs. The photo can be JPG, PNG, HEIC (iPhone photos), WebP, TIFF, AVIF, BMP or GIF; the program converts it as needed.</li><li>Choose <b>Save</b>, or <b>Save and print</b> to print their card straight away.</li></ol>\n<p>The card preview on the right shows the card as it will print.</p>\n<h3 id=\"position-the-photo\">Position the photo</h3>\n<p>The photo is fitted to the card's photo frame automatically: lined up by the face when <b>Line up faces on every card</b> is on in <b>Settings</b>, otherwise as the template says (filling the frame, or showing the whole photo). To adjust one person's photo:</p>\n<ul><li>Drag the photo on the card preview to move it.</li><li>Use <b>Zoom</b>, or scroll on the photo, to zoom in or out. Zoom snaps to filling the frame exactly, so no gap is left down a side. Zoomed right out, the whole photo shows and nothing is cut off.</li><li>Choose <b>Whole photo</b> to show all of it in one step, <b>Reset position</b> to go back to the template's fit, or <b>Remove photo</b> to take it off.</li><li>Choose <b>Enhance</b> to improve a dark, dull or grainy photo. Choose <b>Undo enhance</b> to put it back as it was. When <b>Enhance photos that need it</b> is on in <b>Settings</b>, a photo you add is enhanced straight away if it needs it, and the button shows <b>Undo enhance</b>.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Enhance</b> works only with what's in the photo:</p>\n<ul><li>It sets the darkest and lightest parts to black and white, which fixes a photo that's dull, washed out or too dark all over.</li><li>It smooths grain from a photo taken in poor light, but keeps edges sharp. A clean photo isn't smoothed.</li><li>It brings out detail in shadows.</li><li>It sharpens a little.</li></ul>\n<p>It doesn't change colours: a light blue backdrop and a blue tint look the same to the program, so correcting the tint would also turn faces orange. It doesn't lighten faces either: a face in shadow and darker skin look the same to the program, and a person's skin must stay as it is. It doesn't invent detail, so the photo still looks like the person. It can't fix a photo that's out of focus or very small: retake those.</p>\n<p>Any space around a photo that doesn't fill the frame shows the frame's colour, set in the template.</p>\n<h3 id=\"line-up-faces\">Line up faces</h3>\n<p>When <b>Line up faces on every card</b> is on in <b>Settings</b> (under <b>Photos</b>), everyone's face is the same size and in the same place on their card, however their photo was taken: close up or from further away, centred or off to one side. Their eyes are lined up across the cards, with the head filling about three quarters of the frame, as on a passport photo. It's off to start.</p>\n<ul><li>The program finds each face when the photo is added, whether one person at a time, by <a href=\"#/help/importing-people-from-a-spreadsheet\">importing</a> or by <a href=\"#/help/updating-people-in-bulk\">updating in bulk</a>. It only finds where the eyes are: it never changes the photo, and nothing is sent anywhere. The face finder is part of the program and runs on this PC, with no internet connection needed.</li><li>People whose photos were added while it was off need their faces found once. Choose <b>Line up faces</b> above the list of people (it shows how many). They're done one at a time with a progress bar.</li><li>A photo cropped tightly around the face doesn't have enough around it to move the face into place. The program first makes it a little bigger (the face up to 15% larger than everyone else's), which gives it room. If that's still not enough, the part of the frame the photo doesn't reach is filled with a soft blur of the colours along the photo's edge, so the backdrop just carries on. Nothing from inside the photo is copied, and the person isn't changed. Only an extreme close-up stops short, with a bigger face.</li><li>For the best results, use photos with the head and shoulders and some space around the head, taken front on.</li><li>If no face is found (the person is side on or turned away, the face is very small in the photo, or there's no face), the photo is fitted as the template says and the person shows <b>Face not found</b> in the list. A face that's found but turned to one side shows <b>Face turned away</b>, and one too tightly cropped to line up shows <b>Photo too close-up</b>. See <a href=\"#/help/people\">People who need checking</a>.</li><li>You can still move and zoom anyone's photo by hand. <b>Reset position</b> puts it back to the same face size.</li><li>Turning it off puts every photo back as the template says.</li></ul>\n<p>Photos are shrunk to a sensible size when you add them, so large camera photos are fine.</p>\n<h3 id=\"find-people\">Find people</h3>\n<ul><li>Type in <b>Search</b> to find people by name or any detail shown in the list.</li><li>Use <b>Show</b> to list only people who <b>Needs checking</b> (see below), <b>Active cards</b>, <b>Lost or stolen</b>, <b>Returned</b>, <b>Expired or expiring</b>, or people who were <b>Never printed</b>.</li></ul>\n<p>The list shows each person's photo, details, when their card was last printed and how many cards they've had.</p>\n<h3 id=\"people-who-need-checking\">People who need checking</h3>\n<p>A label under a person's name says when their photo needs checking before you print their card:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Meaning</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>No photo</td><td>They have no photo yet</td><td>Add one, or import their photos with <a href=\"#/help/updating-people-in-bulk\">Updating people in bulk</a></td></tr><tr><td>Face not found</td><td><b>Line up faces</b> is on, but no face was found, so the photo is fitted as the template says</td><td>Edit them to move or zoom the photo by hand, or give them a clearer, front-on photo</td></tr><tr><td>Face turned away</td><td>The face is turned to one side (side on, or clearly three-quarter), so it may not line up like everyone else's, and isn't a front-on ID photo</td><td>Give them a front-on photo</td></tr><tr><td>Photo too close-up</td><td>The photo is cropped so tightly around the face that it can't be made the same size as everyone else's</td><td>Give them a photo with the head and shoulders and some space around the head</td></tr><tr><td>Photo too small</td><td>The photo has too few pixels for the card's photo frame, so it would print soft</td><td>Give them a larger photo, such as one straight from the camera</td></tr><tr><td>Poor photo</td><td>The photo is still clearly dull, dark or grainy</td><td>Try <b>Enhance</b>, or give them a better photo</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Choose <b>Show</b> and then <b>Needs checking</b> to list just these people. At the end of an import or bulk update, the program also lists anyone whose new photo needs checking, grouped by problem; choose <b>Show who needs checking</b> to see them in the list. Photos added before version 3.0.15 aren't checked for these, apart from <b>No photo</b>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"card-status\">Card status</h3>\n<p>When a card is lost or handed back, open the person and set <b>Card</b> to <b>Lost or stolen</b> or <b>Returned</b>. The person's card history is shown underneath. Printing them a new card makes their card active again, after asking you to confirm.</p>\n<p>If you've added an <a href=\"#/help/person-fields\">expiry date field</a>, cards that have expired or expire within 30 days are marked in the list.</p>\n<h3 id=\"edit-or-delete-people\">Edit or delete people</h3>\n<ul><li>Choose <b>Edit</b> on a person to change their details or photo.</li><li>To delete people, tick them and choose <b>Delete</b>. Their details and photos are removed.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"export-people\">Export people</h3>\n<p>Choose <b>Export people</b> to download the client's people as a CSV file that opens in Excel. Photos aren't included. The file can be <a href=\"#/help/importing-people-from-a-spreadsheet\">imported</a> again later.</p>",
  "text": "People are the card holders in a client. Open a client to see their People tab. Add a person Open the client and choose Add person. Type their first and last name, and any other details the card shows. Drop their ID photo onto the photo box, or click it to choose a photo. The box shows the photo size the template needs. The photo can be JPG, PNG, HEIC (iPhone photos), WebP, TIFF, AVIF, BMP or GIF; the program converts it as needed. Choose Save, or Save and print to print their card straight away. The card preview on the right shows the card as it will print. Position the photo The photo is fitted to the card's photo frame automatically: lined up by the face when Line up faces on every card is on in Settings, otherwise as the template says (filling the frame, or showing the whole photo). To adjust one person's photo: Drag the photo on the card preview to move it. Use Zoom, or scroll on the photo, to zoom in or out. Zoom snaps to filling the frame exactly, so no gap is left down a side. Zoomed right out, the whole photo shows and nothing is cut off. Choose Whole photo to show all of it in one step, Reset position to go back to the template's fit, or Remove photo to take it off. Choose Enhance to improve a dark, dull or grainy photo. Choose Undo enhance to put it back as it was. When Enhance photos that need it is on in Settings, a photo you add is enhanced straight away if it needs it, and the button shows Undo enhance. Enhance works only with what's in the photo: It sets the darkest and lightest parts to black and white, which fixes a photo that's dull, washed out or too dark all over. It smooths grain from a photo taken in poor light, but keeps edges sharp. A clean photo isn't smoothed. It brings out detail in shadows. It sharpens a little. It doesn't change colours: a light blue backdrop and a blue tint look the same to the program, so correcting the tint would also turn faces orange. It doesn't lighten faces either: a face in shadow and darker skin look the same to the program, and a person's skin must stay as it is. It doesn't invent detail, so the photo still looks like the person. It can't fix a photo that's out of focus or very small: retake those. Any space around a photo that doesn't fill the frame shows the frame's colour, set in the template. Line up faces When Line up faces on every card is on in Settings (under Photos), everyone's face is the same size and in the same place on their card, however their photo was taken: close up or from further away, centred or off to one side. Their eyes are lined up across the cards, with the head filling about three quarters of the frame, as on a passport photo. It's off to start. The program finds each face when the photo is added, whether one person at a time, by importing or by updating in bulk. It only finds where the eyes are: it never changes the photo, and nothing is sent anywhere. The face finder is part of the program and runs on this PC, with no internet connection needed. People whose photos were added while it was off need their faces found once. Choose Line up faces above the list of people (it shows how many). They're done one at a time with a progress bar. A photo cropped tightly around the face doesn't have enough around it to move the face into place. The program first makes it a little bigger (the face up to 15% larger than everyone else's), which gives it room. If that's still not enough, the part of the frame the photo doesn't reach is filled with a soft blur of the colours along the photo's edge, so the backdrop just carries on. Nothing from inside the photo is copied, and the person isn't changed. Only an extreme close-up stops short, with a bigger face. For the best results, use photos with the head and shoulders and some space around the head, taken front on. If no face is found (the person is side on or turned away, the face is very small in the photo, or there's no face), the photo is fitted as the template says and the person shows Face not found in the list. A face that's found but turned to one side shows Face turned away, and one too tightly cropped to line up shows Photo too close-up. See People who need checking. You can still move and zoom anyone's photo by hand. Reset position puts it back to the same face size. Turning it off puts every photo back as the template says. Photos are shrunk to a sensible size when you add them, so large camera photos are fine. Find people Type in Search to find people by name or any detail shown in the list. Use Show to list only people who Needs checking (see below), Active cards, Lost or stolen, Returned, Expired or expiring, or people who were Never printed. The list shows each person's photo, details, when their card was last printed and how many cards they've had. People who need checking A label under a person's name says when their photo needs checking before you print their card: Label Meaning What to do No photo They have no photo yet Add one, or import their photos with Updating people in bulk Face not found Line up faces is on, but no face was found, so the photo is fitted as the template says Edit them to move or zoom the photo by hand, or give them a clearer, front-on photo Face turned away The face is turned to one side (side on, or clearly three-quarter), so it may not line up like everyone else's, and isn't a front-on ID photo Give them a front-on photo Photo too close-up The photo is cropped so tightly around the face that it can't be made the same size as everyone else's Give them a photo with the head and shoulders and some space around the head Photo too small The photo has too few pixels for the card's photo frame, so it would print soft Give them a larger photo, such as one straight from the camera Poor photo The photo is still clearly dull, dark or grainy Try Enhance, or give them a better photo Choose Show and then Needs checking to list just these people. At the end of an import or bulk update, the program also lists anyone whose new photo needs checking, grouped by problem; choose Show who needs checking to see them in the list. Photos added before version 3.0.15 aren't checked for these, apart from No photo. Card status When a card is lost or handed back, open the person and set Card to Lost or stolen or Returned. The person's card history is shown underneath. Printing them a new card makes their card active again, after asking you to confirm. If you've added an expiry date field, cards that have expired or expire within 30 days are marked in the list. Edit or delete people Choose Edit on a person to change their details or photo. To delete people, tick them and choose Delete. Their details and photos are removed. Export people Choose Export people to download the client's people as a CSV file that opens in Excel. Photos aren't included. The file can be imported again later."
 },
 {
  "id": "importing-people-from-a-spreadsheet",
  "title": "Importing people from a spreadsheet",
  "summary": "Add many people at once from a CSV file, with their ID photos matched to them automatically.",
  "html": "<p>Add many people at once from a CSV file, with their ID photos matched to them automatically.</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-fill-in-the-csv\">1. Fill in the CSV</h3>\n<ol><li>Open the client and choose <b>Import from CSV</b>.</li><li>Choose <b>Download blank CSV</b>. It has a column for each detail the client's card uses.</li><li>Open it in Excel or any spreadsheet program and add one person per row.</li><li>Save it as <b>CSV</b>.</li></ol>\n<p>Dates can be written as 31/03/2027 or 2027-03-31.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-name-the-photos\">2. Name the photos</h3>\n<p>Name each photo after the person: their first name, then their last name, next to each other (last name first works too). Other words before or after the name are fine. Capitals, spaces, dashes and underscores don't matter, so for John Smith any of these match:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Photo file names that match John Smith</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>John Smith.jpg</code>, <code>JOHN SMITH.jpg</code>, <code>john smith.jpg</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>JohnSmith.jpg</code>, <code>JOHNSMITH.jpg</code>, <code>johnsmith.jpg</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>John_Smith.jpg</code>, <code>john-smith.jpg</code>, <code>Smith John.jpg</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>John Smith ID Photo Good.jpg</code>, <code>0042 John Smith.jpg</code>, <code>ID - SMITH John 2026.heic</code></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<ul><li>The two names must be next to each other: <code>John Paul Smith.jpg</code> doesn't match John Smith.</li><li>If one photo is named just the name (<code>John Smith.jpg</code>) and another has other words too (<code>John Smith old.jpg</code>), the one named just the name is used.</li><li>If two photos both have other words (<code>John Smith good.jpg</code> and <code>John Smith bad.jpg</code>), neither is used, because the program can't tell which one you want. Remove the one you don't want from the folder.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>Macrons and accents don't matter either, so <code>Jane Doe.jpg</code> matches Jāne Dōe.</li><li>Photos can be JPG, PNG, HEIC (iPhone photos), WebP, TIFF, AVIF, BMP or GIF, of any size or shape, and can be mixed. The program converts them as needed. Each one is fitted to the card's photo frame as the template says (filling it, or showing the whole photo), or <a href=\"#/help/people\">lined up by the face</a> when that's on in <b>Settings</b>, and you can adjust any of them afterwards.</li><li>The photo size the template needs is shown under <b>Add the ID photos</b>, such as <i>At least 360 × 480 px · 30.5 × 40.6 mm · 3:4 portrait</i>. Photos of that shape fill the frame without being trimmed.</li><li>Only the file name matters. Nothing needs to be written on the photo.</li></ul>\n<p>The CSV doesn't need a photo column: each person's photo is found by their name. (A CSV that has an <b>ID Photo</b> column, such as one exported from the program, still works: the photo named there is used.)</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-upload-and-review\">3. Upload and review</h3>\n<ol><li>Choose <b>Upload CSV</b> and pick your CSV file.</li><li>Choose <b>Choose folder</b> and pick the folder with the photos, or <b>Choose files</b> to pick them one by one. Every type of photo is ticked to start with, and the number of each type found is shown beside it: untick a type to leave those files out.</li><li>To improve photos that are off, tick <b>Enhance photos that need it</b> (it's ticked to start when that's on in <b>Settings</b>). Each photo is checked, and only those that are dull, washed out, too dark all over or grainy are enhanced; the rest are kept as they are. (A file's size doesn't show this: it mostly depends on how many pixels the photo has.) The thumbnails in the list show how each photo will look, and the enhanced ones are outlined. See <a href=\"#/help/people\">People</a> for what Enhance does.</li><li>Check the list. Each row shows the photo it matched and its status:</li></ol>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ready</td><td>Will be imported with its photo</td></tr><tr><td>No photo</td><td>Will be imported without a photo</td></tr><tr><td>Photo not found</td><td>The CSV names a photo you didn't choose; held back</td></tr><tr><td>More than one photo: no photo</td><td>More than one photo has the person's name (they're listed in the row). Imported without a photo: add it by editing them, or remove the extra photos from the folder and choose it again</td></tr><tr><td>Shared photo: no photo</td><td>The photo has the name of more than one person: two rows with the same name, or two names in one photo name. Each is imported without a photo; add each one's photo by editing them</td></tr><tr><td>Name missing</td><td>The row has no name; held back</td></tr><tr><td>Already in client</td><td>Someone with that name is already in the client; unticked</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<ol><li>Untick anyone you don't want, then choose <b>Import</b>. People are imported one at a time, so even 1,000 people work through steadily without slowing the PC. A window shows a progress bar, who is being imported (<i>Importing 312 of 1,000: Jane Doe</i>) and how many photos have been enhanced. <b>Stop</b> stops after the person being imported; everyone before them stays imported. At the end, the program lists anyone who couldn't be imported, with the reason, and anyone whose photo needs checking (such as <b>Face not found</b> or <b>Photo too small</b>), grouped by problem. See <a href=\"#/help/people\">People who need checking</a>.</li><li>The program offers to print the new people's cards straight away.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"note\"><b>Note:</b> Importing the same CSV again skips everyone already added, so you can fix a few rows and import again.</div>",
  "text": "Add many people at once from a CSV file, with their ID photos matched to them automatically. 1. Fill in the CSV Open the client and choose Import from CSV. Choose Download blank CSV. It has a column for each detail the client's card uses. Open it in Excel or any spreadsheet program and add one person per row. Save it as CSV. Dates can be written as 31/03/2027 or 2027-03-31. 2. Name the photos Name each photo after the person: their first name, then their last name, next to each other (last name first works too). Other words before or after the name are fine. Capitals, spaces, dashes and underscores don't matter, so for John Smith any of these match: Photo file names that match John Smith John Smith.jpg, JOHN SMITH.jpg, john smith.jpg JohnSmith.jpg, JOHNSMITH.jpg, johnsmith.jpg John_Smith.jpg, john-smith.jpg, Smith John.jpg John Smith ID Photo Good.jpg, 0042 John Smith.jpg, ID - SMITH John 2026.heic The two names must be next to each other: John Paul Smith.jpg doesn't match John Smith. If one photo is named just the name (John Smith.jpg) and another has other words too (John Smith old.jpg), the one named just the name is used. If two photos both have other words (John Smith good.jpg and John Smith bad.jpg), neither is used, because the program can't tell which one you want. Remove the one you don't want from the folder. Macrons and accents don't matter either, so Jane Doe.jpg matches Jāne Dōe. Photos can be JPG, PNG, HEIC (iPhone photos), WebP, TIFF, AVIF, BMP or GIF, of any size or shape, and can be mixed. The program converts them as needed. Each one is fitted to the card's photo frame as the template says (filling it, or showing the whole photo), or lined up by the face when that's on in Settings, and you can adjust any of them afterwards. The photo size the template needs is shown under Add the ID photos, such as At least 360 × 480 px · 30.5 × 40.6 mm · 3:4 portrait. Photos of that shape fill the frame without being trimmed. Only the file name matters. Nothing needs to be written on the photo. The CSV doesn't need a photo column: each person's photo is found by their name. (A CSV that has an ID Photo column, such as one exported from the program, still works: the photo named there is used.) 3. Upload and review Choose Upload CSV and pick your CSV file. Choose Choose folder and pick the folder with the photos, or Choose files to pick them one by one. Every type of photo is ticked to start with, and the number of each type found is shown beside it: untick a type to leave those files out. To improve photos that are off, tick Enhance photos that need it (it's ticked to start when that's on in Settings). Each photo is checked, and only those that are dull, washed out, too dark all over or grainy are enhanced; the rest are kept as they are. (A file's size doesn't show this: it mostly depends on how many pixels the photo has.) The thumbnails in the list show how each photo will look, and the enhanced ones are outlined. See People for what Enhance does. Check the list. Each row shows the photo it matched and its status: Status Meaning Ready Will be imported with its photo No photo Will be imported without a photo Photo not found The CSV names a photo you didn't choose; held back More than one photo: no photo More than one photo has the person's name (they're listed in the row). Imported without a photo: add it by editing them, or remove the extra photos from the folder and choose it again Shared photo: no photo The photo has the name of more than one person: two rows with the same name, or two names in one photo name. Each is imported without a photo; add each one's photo by editing them Name missing The row has no name; held back Already in client Someone with that name is already in the client; unticked Untick anyone you don't want, then choose Import. People are imported one at a time, so even 1,000 people work through steadily without slowing the PC. A window shows a progress bar, who is being imported (Importing 312 of 1,000: Jane Doe) and how many photos have been enhanced. Stop stops after the person being imported; everyone before them stays imported. At the end, the program lists anyone who couldn't be imported, with the reason, and anyone whose photo needs checking (such as Face not found or Photo too small), grouped by problem. See People who need checking. The program offers to print the new people's cards straight away. Note: Importing the same CSV again skips everyone already added, so you can fix a few rows and import again."
 },
 {
  "id": "updating-people-in-bulk",
  "title": "Updating people in bulk",
  "summary": "Change details or add photos for many people already in a client at once, for example importing 1,000 people now and their photos later. A bulk update only changes people who are already there: it never adds or deletes anyone.",
  "html": "<p>Change details or add photos for many people already in a client at once, for example importing 1,000 people now and their photos later. A bulk update only changes people who are already there: it never adds or deletes anyone.</p>\n<ol><li>Open the client and choose <b>Bulk update</b>.</li><li>To change details, under <b>Upload a CSV of changes</b> choose <b>Upload CSV</b> and pick a CSV with <b>First Name</b>, <b>Last Name</b> and a column for each detail to change. The easiest way is to choose <b>Export people</b>, change the cells you want in Excel, and save it as CSV.</li><li>To add or replace photos, under <b>Add the ID photos</b> choose <b>Choose folder</b> or <b>Choose files</b>. They're matched by name exactly as when <a href=\"#/help/importing-people-from-a-spreadsheet\">importing</a>: <code>John Smith.heic</code> and <code>John Smith ID Photo.jpg</code> go to John Smith. Untick any type of photo to leave it out, and tick <b>Enhance photos that need it</b> to improve the ones that are off.</li><li>Check the list. It shows each person who will change, what changes (such as <i>Position: Driver → Supervisor</i>, <i>Adds photo</i> or <i>Replaces photo</i>) and the new photo:</li></ol>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ready</td><td>Will be updated</td></tr><tr><td>No changes</td><td>The CSV row is the same as the person's details already, and there's no new photo</td></tr><tr><td>Not found</td><td>Nobody in the client has that name; the row is left out</td></tr><tr><td>Same name twice: skipped</td><td>Two people in the client have that name, so the program can't tell whose row or photo it is. Update them by editing them</td></tr><tr><td>In the CSV twice: skipped</td><td>The CSV has two rows for the same person, so neither is used</td></tr><tr><td>More than one photo: skipped</td><td>More than one photo has the person's name (they're listed in the row), so it isn't clear which one to use</td></tr><tr><td>Shared photo: skipped</td><td>The photo has the name of more than one person, so it isn't clear whose it is</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>If a person has CSV changes as well, those changes are still made and only the photo is left as it is.</p>\n<ol><li>Untick anyone you don't want, then choose <b>Update</b> (the button shows how many people). As when importing, people are updated one at a time with a progress window, and at the end the program lists anyone who couldn't be updated and anyone whose new photo needs checking.</li></ol>\n<p>You can use either step on its own: just photos, or just a CSV.</p>\n<ul><li>A blank cell leaves that detail as it is. Names are never changed: they're how people are found.</li><li>A new photo replaces the person's old one, and its position on the card goes back to the template's fit.</li><li>Photos that aren't used are listed under the table (<i>3 photos not used</i>), so you can check their names.</li></ul>",
  "text": "Change details or add photos for many people already in a client at once, for example importing 1,000 people now and their photos later. A bulk update only changes people who are already there: it never adds or deletes anyone. Open the client and choose Bulk update. To change details, under Upload a CSV of changes choose Upload CSV and pick a CSV with First Name, Last Name and a column for each detail to change. The easiest way is to choose Export people, change the cells you want in Excel, and save it as CSV. To add or replace photos, under Add the ID photos choose Choose folder or Choose files. They're matched by name exactly as when importing: John Smith.heic and John Smith ID Photo.jpg go to John Smith. Untick any type of photo to leave it out, and tick Enhance photos that need it to improve the ones that are off. Check the list. It shows each person who will change, what changes (such as Position: Driver → Supervisor, Adds photo or Replaces photo) and the new photo: Status Meaning Ready Will be updated No changes The CSV row is the same as the person's details already, and there's no new photo Not found Nobody in the client has that name; the row is left out Same name twice: skipped Two people in the client have that name, so the program can't tell whose row or photo it is. Update them by editing them In the CSV twice: skipped The CSV has two rows for the same person, so neither is used More than one photo: skipped More than one photo has the person's name (they're listed in the row), so it isn't clear which one to use Shared photo: skipped The photo has the name of more than one person, so it isn't clear whose it is If a person has CSV changes as well, those changes are still made and only the photo is left as it is. Untick anyone you don't want, then choose Update (the button shows how many people). As when importing, people are updated one at a time with a progress window, and at the end the program lists anyone who couldn't be updated and anyone whose new photo needs checking. You can use either step on its own: just photos, or just a CSV. A blank cell leaves that detail as it is. Names are never changed: they're how people are found. A new photo replaces the person's old one, and its position on the card goes back to the template's fit. Photos that aren't used are listed under the table (3 photos not used), so you can check their names."
 },
 {
  "id": "printing-cards",
  "title": "Printing cards",
  "summary": "Print one card or a whole selection, check proofs first, or print a test card.",
  "html": "<p>Print one card or a whole selection, check proofs first, or print a test card.</p>\n<h3 id=\"print-one-persons-card\">Print one person's card</h3>\n<p>Choose <b>Print</b> on the person's row, or <b>Save and print</b> when adding or editing them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"print-many-cards\">Print many cards</h3>\n<ol><li>Open the client's <b>People</b> tab.</li><li>Tick the people, or tick the box at the top of the list to select everyone shown.</li><li>Check the <b>Card template</b>, and the <b>Sides</b> if the card has a back.</li><li>Choose <b>Print</b> (the button shows how many cards). The cards go to the <a href=\"#/help/the-print-queue\">print queue</a> and print one at a time.</li></ol>\n<p>Before printing, the program checks and asks you to confirm when:</p>\n<ul><li>someone has no photo (their photo area prints blank),</li><li>a card is marked lost, returned or expired,</li><li>the ribbon or film may run out before the cards are done.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"proof-sheets\">Proof sheets</h3>\n<p>To let a client check their cards before you print, tick the people and choose <b>Proof sheet</b>. It opens a page of their cards at actual size, to print or save as a PDF.</p>\n<h3 id=\"print-a-test-card\">Print a test card</h3>\n<p>In <b>Settings</b>, choose <b>Print a test card</b> to check the printer works. It uses your first template, or a plain test pattern if you have none yet.</p>",
  "text": "Print one card or a whole selection, check proofs first, or print a test card. Print one person's card Choose Print on the person's row, or Save and print when adding or editing them. Print many cards Open the client's People tab. Tick the people, or tick the box at the top of the list to select everyone shown. Check the Card template, and the Sides if the card has a back. Choose Print (the button shows how many cards). The cards go to the print queue and print one at a time. Before printing, the program checks and asks you to confirm when: someone has no photo (their photo area prints blank), a card is marked lost, returned or expired, the ribbon or film may run out before the cards are done. Proof sheets To let a client check their cards before you print, tick the people and choose Proof sheet. It opens a page of their cards at actual size, to print or save as a PDF. Print a test card In Settings, choose Print a test card to check the printer works. It uses your first template, or a plain test pattern if you have none yet."
 },
 {
  "id": "the-print-queue",
  "title": "The print queue",
  "summary": "The print queue shows every batch of cards: waiting, printing and finished. Choose Print queue in the sidebar. The number beside it counts cards still to print.",
  "html": "<p>The print queue shows every batch of cards: waiting, printing and finished. Choose <b>Print queue</b> in the sidebar. The number beside it counts cards still to print.</p>\n<p>While cards print, a bar along the bottom of every screen shows progress, with <b>Pause</b> and <b>Open queue</b>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"batches-and-cards\">Batches and cards</h3>\n<p>Each time you print, the cards become a batch. Click a batch to open it and see each card:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Card status</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Queued</td><td>Waiting its turn</td></tr><tr><td>Sending, Printing</td><td>Going to the printer now</td></tr><tr><td>Needs attention</td><td>The printer needs you, for example it's out of cards</td></tr><tr><td>Printed</td><td>Done</td></tr><tr><td>Check output</td><td>The program stopped while this card was printing; check whether it came out</td></tr><tr><td>Failed</td><td>It didn't print</td></tr><tr><td>Cancelled</td><td>You cancelled it</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"pause-and-resume\">Pause and resume</h3>\n<ul><li>Choose <b>Pause</b> to stop after the current card. Choose <b>Resume printing</b> to carry on.</li><li>If <b>Pause the queue on printer problems</b> is on in Settings, the queue waits while the printer has a problem and carries on when it's fixed.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"fix-a-card-that-didnt-print\">Fix a card that didn't print</h3>\n<p>For a card marked <b>Check output</b> or <b>Failed</b>, look in the printer's output tray:</p>\n<ul><li>If the card came out properly, choose <b>Mark printed</b>.</li><li>If not, choose <b>Reprint</b>.</li></ul>\n<p>A card is never printed twice without you choosing to.</p>\n<h3 id=\"if-the-pc-restarts-or-the-printer-is-unplugged\">If the PC restarts or the printer is unplugged</h3>\n<p>The program saves each card's progress as it prints, so nothing is lost if the PC restarts, the power goes off, or the program closes in the middle of a batch.</p>\n<ol><li>Open the program again, or wait for it to start if it starts with Windows. A notification says how many cards need checking.</li><li>Open the <b>Print queue</b>. Cards that finished are still <b>Printed</b>, and the cards that hadn't started are still waiting, in order.</li><li>The card that was printing when it stopped is marked <b>Check output</b>. Look in the printer's output tray and choose <b>Mark printed</b> if it came out properly, or <b>Reprint</b> if not.</li><li>Choose <b>Resume printing</b>. Printing carries on from the next card.</li></ol>\n<p>If the printer is unplugged or switched off in the middle of a card, the card shows <b>Needs attention</b> and nothing more is sent. Plug the printer back in, check that card the same way, then choose <b>Resume printing</b> if the queue has paused. If the printer drops out between cards, the queue waits and carries on by itself when the printer is back.</p>\n<h3 id=\"more-actions\">More actions</h3>\n<ul><li>The up arrow on a waiting card prints it next in its batch.</li><li>The cross on a card cancels it. <b>Cancel remaining</b> cancels the rest of the batch.</li><li><b>Reprint failed &amp; cancelled</b> tries those cards again.</li><li><b>Remove batch</b> takes a batch off the list. <b>Clear finished</b> removes all finished batches.</li></ul>",
  "text": "The print queue shows every batch of cards: waiting, printing and finished. Choose Print queue in the sidebar. The number beside it counts cards still to print. While cards print, a bar along the bottom of every screen shows progress, with Pause and Open queue. Batches and cards Each time you print, the cards become a batch. Click a batch to open it and see each card: Card status Meaning Queued Waiting its turn Sending, Printing Going to the printer now Needs attention The printer needs you, for example it's out of cards Printed Done Check output The program stopped while this card was printing; check whether it came out Failed It didn't print Cancelled You cancelled it Pause and resume Choose Pause to stop after the current card. Choose Resume printing to carry on. If Pause the queue on printer problems is on in Settings, the queue waits while the printer has a problem and carries on when it's fixed. Fix a card that didn't print For a card marked Check output or Failed, look in the printer's output tray: If the card came out properly, choose Mark printed. If not, choose Reprint. A card is never printed twice without you choosing to. If the PC restarts or the printer is unplugged The program saves each card's progress as it prints, so nothing is lost if the PC restarts, the power goes off, or the program closes in the middle of a batch. Open the program again, or wait for it to start if it starts with Windows. A notification says how many cards need checking. Open the Print queue. Cards that finished are still Printed, and the cards that hadn't started are still waiting, in order. The card that was printing when it stopped is marked Check output. Look in the printer's output tray and choose Mark printed if it came out properly, or Reprint if not. Choose Resume printing. Printing carries on from the next card. If the printer is unplugged or switched off in the middle of a card, the card shows Needs attention and nothing more is sent. Plug the printer back in, check that card the same way, then choose Resume printing if the queue has paused. If the printer drops out between cards, the queue waits and carries on by itself when the printer is back. More actions The up arrow on a waiting card prints it next in its batch. The cross on a card cancels it. Cancel remaining cancels the rest of the batch. Reprint failed & cancelled tries those cards again. Remove batch takes a batch off the list. Clear finished removes all finished batches."
 },
 {
  "id": "dual-sided-cards",
  "title": "Dual-sided cards",
  "summary": "Cards with a back can print both sides in one pass on a printer with a flipper module, or in two passes on a printer without one.",
  "html": "<p>Cards with a back can print both sides in one pass on a printer with a flipper module, or in two passes on a printer without one.</p>\n<h3 id=\"set-it-up\">Set it up</h3>\n<ol><li>In <b>Settings</b>, under <b>Printing</b>, set <b>Card type</b> to <b>Dual-sided</b>.</li><li>Under <b>How the back is printed</b>, choose <b>Automatically (printer flips)</b> if your printer has a flipper module, or <b>Manually (flip the cards)</b> if it doesn't.</li><li>Turn on <b>Print a back</b> in the card template. See <a href=\"#/help/card-templates\">Card templates</a>.</li></ol>\n<p>If the printer reports it has no flipper, Settings warns you to choose <b>Manually (flip the cards)</b>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"print-the-backs-by-hand\">Print the backs by hand</h3>\n<ol><li>Print the cards with <b>Sides</b> set to <b>Front only</b>.</li><li>When the fronts are done, the batch in the print queue shows <b>Print backs</b>.</li><li>Take the printed cards from the output hopper, keeping them in order.</li><li>Flip the stack and load it into the input hopper.</li><li>Choose <b>Print backs</b>. Tick <b>Reverse order</b> if flipping the stack reversed the order of the cards.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"note\"><b>Tip:</b> If the backs print upside down, turn on <b>Rotate back side 180°</b> in Settings and reprint them.</div>",
  "text": "Cards with a back can print both sides in one pass on a printer with a flipper module, or in two passes on a printer without one. Set it up In Settings, under Printing, set Card type to Dual-sided. Under How the back is printed, choose Automatically (printer flips) if your printer has a flipper module, or Manually (flip the cards) if it doesn't. Turn on Print a back in the card template. See Card templates. If the printer reports it has no flipper, Settings warns you to choose Manually (flip the cards). Print the backs by hand Print the cards with Sides set to Front only. When the fronts are done, the batch in the print queue shows Print backs. Take the printed cards from the output hopper, keeping them in order. Flip the stack and load it into the input hopper. Choose Print backs. Tick Reverse order if flipping the stack reversed the order of the cards. Tip: If the backs print upside down, turn on Rotate back side 180° in Settings and reprint them."
 },
 {
  "id": "printer-setup-and-status",
  "title": "Printer setup and status",
  "summary": "Choose your card printer, check its status and supplies, and set how printing behaves.",
  "html": "<p>Choose your card printer, check its status and supplies, and set how printing behaves.</p>\n<h3 id=\"choose-your-printer\">Choose your printer</h3>\n<ol><li>Install your card printer's Windows driver first, from the printer maker.</li><li>In <b>Settings</b>, under <b>Printer</b>, choose your printer in <b>Card printer</b>. HID FARGO printers are marked with a star, and one is chosen for you if it's the only one found.</li><li>Choose <b>Refresh list</b> if you've just installed the printer.</li><li>Choose <b>Print a test card</b> to check it works.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Printing preferences</b> opens the printer driver's own settings, and <b>Windows print queue</b> opens the Windows queue for the printer.</p>\n<h3 id=\"printer-status\">Printer status</h3>\n<p>The status in the sidebar shows <b>Ready</b>, busy or a problem, with the ribbon level on printers that report it. Click it to see:</p>\n<ul><li>the printer, its driver and port,</li><li>the ribbon and film levels, card counter and whether a flipper is fitted (HID FARGO printers),</li><li>any problems and what to do about them,</li><li>jobs waiting in Windows, and recent printer events.</li></ul>\n<p>A banner across the top of every screen shows the most important printer problem. See <a href=\"#/help/printer-alerts\">Printer alerts</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"printing-options\">Printing options</h3>\n<p>In <b>Settings</b>, under <b>Printing</b>:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Start printing automatically</td><td>Prints as soon as cards are added to the queue</td></tr><tr><td>Pause the queue on printer problems</td><td>Waits while the printer has a problem, instead of sending more cards</td></tr><tr><td>Pause between cards (seconds)</td><td>Waits between cards, for printers that need a rest</td></tr><tr><td>Flag a card as slow after (minutes)</td><td>Warns you when a card takes longer than this</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Under <b>Photos</b>, both off to start:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Enhance photos that need it</td><td>Every new photo, whether added one at a time, imported or updated in bulk, is enhanced if it's dull, washed out, too dark all over or grainy. See <a href=\"#/help/people\">Position the photo</a> for what Enhance does</td></tr><tr><td>Line up faces on every card</td><td>Every card's photo is lined up by the face, so faces are the same size and in the same place. See <a href=\"#/help/people\">Line up faces</a></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Under <b>Supplies</b>, set when to warn about a low ribbon or HDP film.</p>\n<h3 id=\"print-adjustments\">Print adjustments</h3>\n<p>Card printers often print lighter and duller than the screen shows. <b>Print adjustments</b>, in <b>Settings</b> under <b>Printer</b>, make up for it on every card. They change only what is sent to the printer: templates, previews and proof sheets stay as designed. To make one template's background, logo or photos darker on screen and on the card, use <b>Darkness</b>, <b>Contrast</b> and <b>Colour</b> in the <a href=\"#/help/card-templates\">template builder</a> instead.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Darkness</td><td>Towards <b>Darker</b>, the in-between shades print darker, such as a pale background or a light photo. White stays white and black stays black.</td></tr><tr><td>Contrast</td><td>Towards <b>More</b>, dark shades get darker and light shades lighter.</td></tr><tr><td>Colour</td><td>Towards <b>Stronger</b>, colours print richer without getting darker.</td></tr><tr><td>Overprint edges</td><td>Prints the card this much larger on every side (up to 2 mm), to cover a thin white edge the printer leaves. That much is trimmed off the design's edges, so keep text and logos away from them.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><b>Automatic</b>: for a printer the program knows, Darkness, Contrast and Colour are set for you, and the switch shows the model, such as <b>Automatic for HID FARGO HDP5000</b>. The program recognises the HID FARGO HDP5000, HDP5600 and HDPii from the printer's or its driver's name. For any other printer they start at 0.</p>\n<p>To set them yourself, turn <b>Automatic</b> off (if it shows), then:</p>\n<ol><li>Move a setting a little, such as Darkness to +15 and Colour to +15.</li><li>Choose <b>Print a test card</b> and compare it with the screen.</li><li>Repeat until the card looks right. <b>Reset</b> goes back to the start: everything at 0, and <b>Automatic</b> on again for a printer the program knows.</li></ol>\n<p>The printer driver has its own colour and image position settings too, under <b>Printing preferences</b>. On HID FARGO printers, start there for pale colours: see below.</p>\n<h3 id=\"colour-and-black-on-hid-fargo-printers\">Colour and black on HID FARGO printers</h3>\n<p>The HID FARGO HDP5000 driver's own settings, under <b>Printing preferences</b>, work together with the program's. They're worth setting once:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Driver setting</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Image Color</b> tab: <b>Dye-Sub Intensity</b></td><td>Pale or washed-out cards. It sets the print head's heat, and more heat puts more dye on the card, which the program can't do. Change it a little at a time.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Toolbox</b>, <b>Advanced Settings</b>: <b>Image Darkness</b></td><td>The same, stored in the printer. Change it in steps of 5.</td></tr><tr><td><b>K Panel Resin</b> tab: <b>Print All Black with K Panel</b></td><td>Crisp black text and barcodes. The program sends each side of a card as one picture, so without this the driver prints black as a mix of colours, which looks softer. It prints pure black only, so use pure black (#000000) for text and barcodes. If a photo's darkest parts look speckled, limit it to the text and barcode areas with a defined area.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Image Color</b> tab, <b>Advanced Settings</b>: Contrast and Gamma</td><td>Leave them at 0 if you use the program's Print adjustments, so the two don't add up.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Image Position</b></td><td>Moving the whole picture on the card, up to about 8 mm each way.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Image Transfer</b> tab: <b>Transfer Temperature</b></td><td>Patchy or incomplete transfer onto the card (not pale colours).</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The program's <a href=\"#/help/printer-setup-and-status\">Print adjustments</a> are set automatically for these printers. If cards still look pale, raise <b>Dye-Sub Intensity</b> first, then fine-tune the program's <b>Darkness</b> and <b>Colour</b> with <b>Automatic</b> off. The program's adjustments keep white unprinted and keep pure black only where the design has it, so the resin black panel prints exactly what you designed.</p>\n<h3 id=\"notifications-and-sounds\">Notifications and sounds</h3>\n<p>Under <b>Operator &amp; alerts</b>:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Operator name</b> is recorded in the print history.</li><li><b>Desktop notifications</b> shows Windows notifications, even while the window is closed: when all the cards are printed, when the printer needs attention while cards are waiting, when a card doesn't print, and when cards need checking after a restart. Click a notification to open the print queue.</li><li><b>Alert sounds</b> plays a sound for the same things while the window is open, when Desktop notifications are off. Windows notifications play the Windows notification sound, which you can change in Windows Settings, under <b>Notifications</b>.</li></ul>",
  "text": "Choose your card printer, check its status and supplies, and set how printing behaves. Choose your printer Install your card printer's Windows driver first, from the printer maker. In Settings, under Printer, choose your printer in Card printer. HID FARGO printers are marked with a star, and one is chosen for you if it's the only one found. Choose Refresh list if you've just installed the printer. Choose Print a test card to check it works. Printing preferences opens the printer driver's own settings, and Windows print queue opens the Windows queue for the printer. Printer status The status in the sidebar shows Ready, busy or a problem, with the ribbon level on printers that report it. Click it to see: the printer, its driver and port, the ribbon and film levels, card counter and whether a flipper is fitted (HID FARGO printers), any problems and what to do about them, jobs waiting in Windows, and recent printer events. A banner across the top of every screen shows the most important printer problem. See Printer alerts. Printing options In Settings, under Printing: Setting Does Start printing automatically Prints as soon as cards are added to the queue Pause the queue on printer problems Waits while the printer has a problem, instead of sending more cards Pause between cards (seconds) Waits between cards, for printers that need a rest Flag a card as slow after (minutes) Warns you when a card takes longer than this Under Photos, both off to start: Setting Does Enhance photos that need it Every new photo, whether added one at a time, imported or updated in bulk, is enhanced if it's dull, washed out, too dark all over or grainy. See Position the photo for what Enhance does Line up faces on every card Every card's photo is lined up by the face, so faces are the same size and in the same place. See Line up faces Under Supplies, set when to warn about a low ribbon or HDP film. Print adjustments Card printers often print lighter and duller than the screen shows. Print adjustments, in Settings under Printer, make up for it on every card. They change only what is sent to the printer: templates, previews and proof sheets stay as designed. To make one template's background, logo or photos darker on screen and on the card, use Darkness, Contrast and Colour in the template builder instead. Setting Does Darkness Towards Darker, the in-between shades print darker, such as a pale background or a light photo. White stays white and black stays black. Contrast Towards More, dark shades get darker and light shades lighter. Colour Towards Stronger, colours print richer without getting darker. Overprint edges Prints the card this much larger on every side (up to 2 mm), to cover a thin white edge the printer leaves. That much is trimmed off the design's edges, so keep text and logos away from them. Automatic: for a printer the program knows, Darkness, Contrast and Colour are set for you, and the switch shows the model, such as Automatic for HID FARGO HDP5000. The program recognises the HID FARGO HDP5000, HDP5600 and HDPii from the printer's or its driver's name. For any other printer they start at 0. To set them yourself, turn Automatic off (if it shows), then: Move a setting a little, such as Darkness to +15 and Colour to +15. Choose Print a test card and compare it with the screen. Repeat until the card looks right. Reset goes back to the start: everything at 0, and Automatic on again for a printer the program knows. The printer driver has its own colour and image position settings too, under Printing preferences. On HID FARGO printers, start there for pale colours: see below. Colour and black on HID FARGO printers The HID FARGO HDP5000 driver's own settings, under Printing preferences, work together with the program's. They're worth setting once: Driver setting Use it for Image Color tab: Dye-Sub Intensity Pale or washed-out cards. It sets the print head's heat, and more heat puts more dye on the card, which the program can't do. Change it a little at a time. Toolbox, Advanced Settings: Image Darkness The same, stored in the printer. Change it in steps of 5. K Panel Resin tab: Print All Black with K Panel Crisp black text and barcodes. The program sends each side of a card as one picture, so without this the driver prints black as a mix of colours, which looks softer. It prints pure black only, so use pure black (#000000) for text and barcodes. If a photo's darkest parts look speckled, limit it to the text and barcode areas with a defined area. Image Color tab, Advanced Settings: Contrast and Gamma Leave them at 0 if you use the program's Print adjustments, so the two don't add up. Image Position Moving the whole picture on the card, up to about 8 mm each way. Image Transfer tab: Transfer Temperature Patchy or incomplete transfer onto the card (not pale colours). The program's Print adjustments are set automatically for these printers. If cards still look pale, raise Dye-Sub Intensity first, then fine-tune the program's Darkness and Colour with Automatic off. The program's adjustments keep white unprinted and keep pure black only where the design has it, so the resin black panel prints exactly what you designed. Notifications and sounds Under Operator & alerts: Operator name is recorded in the print history. Desktop notifications shows Windows notifications, even while the window is closed: when all the cards are printed, when the printer needs attention while cards are waiting, when a card doesn't print, and when cards need checking after a restart. Click a notification to open the print queue. Alert sounds plays a sound for the same things while the window is open, when Desktop notifications are off. Windows notifications play the Windows notification sound, which you can change in Windows Settings, under Notifications."
 },
 {
  "id": "printer-alerts",
  "title": "Printer alerts",
  "summary": "When the printer has a problem, a banner explains it and the print queue waits if Pause the queue on printer problems is on. Fix the problem, then choose Resume printing if the queue doesn't carry on by itself.",
  "html": "<p>When the printer has a problem, a banner explains it and the print queue waits if <b>Pause the queue on printer problems</b> is on. Fix the problem, then choose <b>Resume printing</b> if the queue doesn't carry on by itself.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Alert</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>No printer selected</td><td>Choose your card printer in Settings</td></tr><tr><td>Printer not found</td><td>Check the printer's driver is installed and the printer chosen in Settings is right</td></tr><tr><td>Printer offline</td><td>Check the USB cable and that the printer is switched on. In Windows, make sure <i>Use Printer Offline</i> isn't ticked</td></tr><tr><td>Ribbon or film out</td><td>Replace or re-seat the ribbon or film, then resume</td></tr><tr><td>Ribbon running low</td><td>Have a replacement ribbon ready</td></tr><tr><td>Ribbon empty / HDP film empty</td><td>Fit a new ribbon or film roll, then resume</td></tr><tr><td>Out of cards</td><td>Load cards into the hopper, then resume</td></tr><tr><td>Card jam</td><td>Open the printer cover, clear the card and close the cover</td></tr><tr><td>Cover open</td><td>Close the printer cover</td></tr><tr><td>Printer needs attention</td><td>Check the message on the printer's own display</td></tr><tr><td>Printer paused in Windows</td><td>Open the Windows print queue and choose Resume</td></tr><tr><td>Printer out of memory</td><td>Restart the printer</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Printer events are kept in <b>Print history</b> on the <b>Printer events</b> tab.</p>",
  "text": "When the printer has a problem, a banner explains it and the print queue waits if Pause the queue on printer problems is on. Fix the problem, then choose Resume printing if the queue doesn't carry on by itself. Alert What to do No printer selected Choose your card printer in Settings Printer not found Check the printer's driver is installed and the printer chosen in Settings is right Printer offline Check the USB cable and that the printer is switched on. In Windows, make sure Use Printer Offline isn't ticked Ribbon or film out Replace or re-seat the ribbon or film, then resume Ribbon running low Have a replacement ribbon ready Ribbon empty / HDP film empty Fit a new ribbon or film roll, then resume Out of cards Load cards into the hopper, then resume Card jam Open the printer cover, clear the card and close the cover Cover open Close the printer cover Printer needs attention Check the message on the printer's own display Printer paused in Windows Open the Windows print queue and choose Resume Printer out of memory Restart the printer Printer events are kept in Print history on the Printer events tab."
 },
 {
  "id": "print-history",
  "title": "Print history",
  "summary": "Every card ever printed is in Print history, with who printed it, the template, and the ribbon level at the time.",
  "html": "<p>Every card ever printed is in <b>Print history</b>, with who printed it, the template, and the ribbon level at the time.</p>\n<h3 id=\"find-cards\">Find cards</h3>\n<ul><li><b>Search</b> by name, batch or operator.</li><li>Choose a <b>Client</b>, a <b>Period</b> (Today, This week, This month, All time, or your own dates) and a <b>Result</b> (Printed, Failed, Check output, Cancelled).</li></ul>\n<p>The totals at the top show cards printed today, this week, this month and in all.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reprint-from-history\">Reprint from history</h3>\n<p>Choose <b>Reprint</b> on a row to print a new card for that person with their current details and the same template.</p>\n<h3 id=\"export\">Export</h3>\n<p>Choose <b>Export to CSV</b> to download the cards shown, to open in Excel.</p>\n<h3 id=\"logs\">Logs</h3>\n<ul><li><b>Printer events</b>: every printer problem and when it cleared.</li><li><b>Service log</b>: the program's own log, for troubleshooting (administrators only).</li></ul>",
  "text": "Every card ever printed is in Print history, with who printed it, the template, and the ribbon level at the time. Find cards Search by name, batch or operator. Choose a Client, a Period (Today, This week, This month, All time, or your own dates) and a Result (Printed, Failed, Check output, Cancelled). The totals at the top show cards printed today, this week, this month and in all. Reprint from history Choose Reprint on a row to print a new card for that person with their current details and the same template. Export Choose Export to CSV to download the cards shown, to open in Excel. Logs Printer events: every printer problem and when it cleared. Service log: the program's own log, for troubleshooting (administrators only)."
 },
 {
  "id": "person-fields",
  "title": "Person fields",
  "summary": "Person fields add your own details to people, such as a department, an expiry date or a card number. Administrators add them in Settings, under Person fields.",
  "html": "<p>Person fields add your own details to people, such as a department, an expiry date or a card number. Administrators add them in <b>Settings</b>, under <b>Person fields</b>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"add-a-field\">Add a field</h3>\n<ol><li>Choose <b>Add field</b>, or a <b>Quick add</b> button: <b>Department</b>, <b>Expiry date</b> or <b>Card number</b>.</li><li>Type the field's <b>Name</b> and choose its <b>Type</b> (see the table below).</li><li>Choose <b>Add field</b>.</li></ol>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Text</td><td>Words, such as a department</td></tr><tr><td>Date</td><td>Dates. Turn on <b>This is the card expiry date</b> to mark expired and expiring cards</td></tr><tr><td>Number that counts up</td><td>Card numbers, with an optional <b>Prefix</b>, the number of <b>Digits</b> and the <b>Next number</b></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The new field appears when adding people, in the CSV import, and in the template builder's <b>Fields</b> list.</p>\n<div class=\"note\"><b>Tip:</b> A <b>Number that counts up</b> fills itself in when you save a person and leave it empty, such as C00001, C00002.</div>\n<h3 id=\"change-or-delete-a-field\">Change or delete a field</h3>\n<p>Choose <b>Edit</b> to rename a field or change its numbering. Deleting a field hides people's values for it, and templates that show it leave that space blank.</p>",
  "text": "Person fields add your own details to people, such as a department, an expiry date or a card number. Administrators add them in Settings, under Person fields. Add a field Choose Add field, or a Quick add button: Department, Expiry date or Card number. Type the field's Name and choose its Type (see the table below). Choose Add field. Type Use Text Words, such as a department Date Dates. Turn on This is the card expiry date to mark expired and expiring cards Number that counts up Card numbers, with an optional Prefix, the number of Digits and the Next number The new field appears when adding people, in the CSV import, and in the template builder's Fields list. Tip: A Number that counts up fills itself in when you save a person and leave it empty, such as C00001, C00002. Change or delete a field Choose Edit to rename a field or change its numbering. Deleting a field hides people's values for it, and templates that show it leave that space blank."
 },
 {
  "id": "users-and-passwords",
  "title": "Users and passwords",
  "summary": "Give each person their own sign-in, choose what they can do, and manage passwords.",
  "html": "<p>Give each person their own sign-in, choose what they can do, and manage passwords.</p>\n<h3 id=\"administrators-and-operators\">Administrators and operators</h3>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Can</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Administrator</td><td>Everything, including templates, settings, users and backups</td></tr><tr><td>Operator</td><td>Add and edit people, import, and print</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"add-a-user\">Add a user</h3>\n<ol><li>In <b>Settings</b>, under <b>Users</b>, choose <b>Add user</b>.</li><li>Type a <b>Username</b>, choose the <b>Role</b>, and type a <b>Temporary password</b> (at least 8 characters).</li><li>Choose <b>Add user</b>. They choose their own password the first time they sign in.</li></ol>\n<p>Change a user's role with the list beside their name. <b>Reset password</b> gives them a new temporary password.</p>\n<h3 id=\"change-your-own-password-or-username\">Change your own password or username</h3>\n<ol><li>In <b>Settings</b>, under <b>Sign-in and security</b>, type your new username or password.</li><li>Type your <b>Current password</b>.</li><li>Choose <b>Save sign-in details</b>.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"staying-signed-in\">Staying signed in</h3>\n<ul><li>The program signs you out after a time without use: 120 minutes unless an administrator changes <b>Sign out after this many minutes of inactivity</b>.</li><li>After too many wrong passwords, signing in pauses for a short time.</li><li>Choose the sign-out button beside your name in the sidebar to sign out.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"forgotten-the-admin-password\">Forgotten the admin password</h3>\n<p>Any administrator can choose <b>Reset password</b> for another user. If nobody can sign in:</p>\n<ol><li>Run <code>&amp;quot;C:\\Program Files\\ID Card Printing Software Free\\ID Card Printing Software.exe&amp;quot; --reset-admin</code></li><li>Windows asks for an administrator's permission. Choose <b>Yes</b>, or enter a Windows administrator's password.</li><li>Sign in as <code>admin</code> with the password <code>admin</code>, and choose a new password.</li></ol>\n<p>Only a Windows administrator on the PC can do this.</p>",
  "text": "Give each person their own sign-in, choose what they can do, and manage passwords. Administrators and operators Role Can Administrator Everything, including templates, settings, users and backups Operator Add and edit people, import, and print Add a user In Settings, under Users, choose Add user. Type a Username, choose the Role, and type a Temporary password (at least 8 characters). Choose Add user. They choose their own password the first time they sign in. Change a user's role with the list beside their name. Reset password gives them a new temporary password. Change your own password or username In Settings, under Sign-in and security, type your new username or password. Type your Current password. Choose Save sign-in details. Staying signed in The program signs you out after a time without use: 120 minutes unless an administrator changes Sign out after this many minutes of inactivity. After too many wrong passwords, signing in pauses for a short time. Choose the sign-out button beside your name in the sidebar to sign out. Forgotten the admin password Any administrator can choose Reset password for another user. If nobody can sign in: Run &quot;C:\\Program Files\\ID Card Printing Software Free\\ID Card Printing Software.exe&quot; --reset-admin Windows asks for an administrator's permission. Choose Yes, or enter a Windows administrator's password. Sign in as admin with the password admin, and choose a new password. Only a Windows administrator on the PC can do this."
 },
 {
  "id": "backups-and-moving-to-a-new-pc",
  "title": "Backups and moving to a new PC",
  "summary": "Everything (people, photos, templates, settings and history) is in one encrypted database on this PC. A backup copies all of it into one .bak file, which can be restored on any PC with this program.",
  "html": "<p>Everything (people, photos, templates, settings and history) is in one encrypted database on this PC. A backup copies all of it into one <code>.bak</code> file, which can be restored on any PC with this program.</p>\n<h3 id=\"make-a-backup\">Make a backup</h3>\n<ol><li>In <b>Settings</b>, under <b>Backup</b>, choose <b>Download backup</b>.</li><li>Leave <b>Protect the backup with a password</b> ticked, then choose a backup password (at least 8 characters) and type it again. For a backup without a password, untick it.</li><li>Choose <b>Download backup</b> and keep the <code>.bak</code> file somewhere safe, such as a USB drive.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"note\"><b>Note:</b> Keep the backup password safe. Without it, the backup can't be restored or opened. A backup without a password can be restored by anyone who has the file, so keep it where only the right people can reach it.</div>\n<h3 id=\"automatic-backups\">Automatic backups</h3>\n<ol><li>In <b>Settings</b>, under <b>Automatic backups</b>, turn on <b>Back up automatically</b>.</li><li>Type the <b>Folder</b> to save to, such as a second drive or a network share.</li><li>Choose <b>Every day</b> or <b>Every week</b>, and how many backups to <b>Keep the newest</b>.</li><li>Choose a backup password, then <b>Save</b>.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Back up now</b> makes one straight away. The card shows when the last backup was made, or why it failed.</p>\n<h3 id=\"restore-a-backup\">Restore a backup</h3>\n<ol><li>In <b>Settings</b>, under <b>Backup</b>, choose <b>Restore from backup</b>.</li><li>Choose the backup file. If it has a backup password, type it.</li><li>Choose <b>Replace with backup</b>.</li></ol>\n<p>Backups made before version 3.0.17 end in <code>.idcbak</code> and restore the same way.</p>\n<p>Everything on this PC is replaced with the backup, and you sign in with the usernames and passwords from the backup.</p>\n<h3 id=\"move-to-a-new-pc\">Move to a new PC</h3>\n<ol><li>Make a backup on the old PC.</li><li>Install the program on the new PC and sign in.</li><li>Restore the backup on the new PC.</li></ol>\n<p>Copying the database file itself doesn't work: it's locked to the PC that made it.</p>",
  "text": "Everything (people, photos, templates, settings and history) is in one encrypted database on this PC. A backup copies all of it into one .bak file, which can be restored on any PC with this program. Make a backup In Settings, under Backup, choose Download backup. Leave Protect the backup with a password ticked, then choose a backup password (at least 8 characters) and type it again. For a backup without a password, untick it. Choose Download backup and keep the .bak file somewhere safe, such as a USB drive. Note: Keep the backup password safe. Without it, the backup can't be restored or opened. A backup without a password can be restored by anyone who has the file, so keep it where only the right people can reach it. Automatic backups In Settings, under Automatic backups, turn on Back up automatically. Type the Folder to save to, such as a second drive or a network share. Choose Every day or Every week, and how many backups to Keep the newest. Choose a backup password, then Save. Back up now makes one straight away. The card shows when the last backup was made, or why it failed. Restore a backup In Settings, under Backup, choose Restore from backup. Choose the backup file. If it has a backup password, type it. Choose Replace with backup. Backups made before version 3.0.17 end in .idcbak and restore the same way. Everything on this PC is replaced with the backup, and you sign in with the usernames and passwords from the backup. Move to a new PC Make a backup on the old PC. Install the program on the new PC and sign in. Restore the backup on the new PC. Copying the database file itself doesn't work: it's locked to the PC that made it."
 },
 {
  "id": "appearance",
  "title": "Appearance",
  "summary": "Administrators can match the program to their business in Settings, under Appearance.",
  "html": "<p>Administrators can match the program to their business in <b>Settings</b>, under <b>Appearance</b>.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Theme</b>: choose a colour scheme, such as Classic blue, Teal or Graphite.</li><li><b>Main colour</b> and <b>Heading colour</b>: pick your own colours. <b>Automatic heading colour</b> goes back to a heading colour that suits the main colour.</li><li><b>Sidebar</b>: <b>Light</b>, or filled with the <b>Main colour</b>.</li><li><b>Company name</b> and <b>Upload logo</b>: shown in the sidebar and on the sign-in screen.</li></ul>",
  "text": "Administrators can match the program to their business in Settings, under Appearance. Theme: choose a colour scheme, such as Classic blue, Teal or Graphite. Main colour and Heading colour: pick your own colours. Automatic heading colour goes back to a heading colour that suits the main colour. Sidebar: Light, or filled with the Main colour. Company name and Upload logo: shown in the sidebar and on the sign-in screen."
 },
 {
  "id": "updates",
  "title": "Updates",
  "summary": "The program checks for new versions by itself.",
  "html": "<p>The program checks for new versions by itself.</p>\n<ol><li>When a new version is out, administrators see <b>Version x.y.z is available</b> at the top of the program.</li><li>Choose <b>What's new</b> to read about it, or <b>Update now</b> to install it.</li><li>Answer <b>Yes</b> if Windows asks for permission.</li></ol>\n<p>The program closes, updates and opens again by itself. An update only adds or changes what it's for: your people, photos, templates, settings and shortcuts stay exactly as they were.</p>\n<p>To check straight away, choose <b>Check for updates</b> in <b>Settings</b>, under <b>About</b>.</p>",
  "text": "The program checks for new versions by itself. When a new version is out, administrators see Version x.y.z is available at the top of the program. Choose What's new to read about it, or Update now to install it. Answer Yes if Windows asks for permission. The program closes, updates and opens again by itself. An update only adds or changes what it's for: your people, photos, templates, settings and shortcuts stay exactly as they were. To check straight away, choose Check for updates in Settings, under About."
 },
 {
  "id": "closing-the-program",
  "title": "Closing the program",
  "summary": "The program keeps running in the background so cards keep printing. Here's how to open, close and start it.",
  "html": "<p>The program keeps running in the background so cards keep printing. Here's how to open, close and start it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"closing-the-window\">Closing the window</h3>\n<p>Closing the window doesn't stop the program. It keeps running in the notification area by the clock, so cards keep printing. Double-click its icon there, or right-click it and choose <b>Open ID Card Printing Software</b>, to open the window again.</p>\n<h3 id=\"closing-the-program-completely\">Closing the program completely</h3>\n<ol><li>In <b>Settings</b>, under <b>Program</b>, choose <b>Close the program</b>.</li><li>Choose <b>Close</b> to confirm.</li></ol>\n<p>Printing stops until the program is opened again from the Start menu. Cards left in the queue print when it's opened.</p>\n<h3 id=\"starting-with-windows\">Starting with Windows</h3>\n<p>The setup asks whether to start the program when anyone signs in to Windows. To change your answer, run the setup again and tick or untick <b>Start automatically when anyone signs in to Windows</b>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"where-your-data-is\">Where your data is</h3>\n<p>Everything is stored in <code>C:\\ProgramData\\ID Card Printing Software</code>, shared by every Windows account on the PC. Whoever signs in to Windows sees the same templates, clients, people and print queue, and still signs in to the program with their own username and password. Updating keeps it, and uninstalling the program leaves it in place.</p>\n<p>The database is encrypted so a copy can't be opened on another PC. Anyone who can sign in to Windows on this PC can reach the file, so give Windows accounts on it only to people you trust with the cards' details.</p>\n<p>Up to version 3.0.11 each Windows account kept its own data. The first time an account runs a newer version, its data moves into the shared folder. If the shared folder already has data of its own, the program asks once which to keep. The other copy stays on the PC as a file.</p>\n<h3 id=\"more-than-one-windows-account-signed-in\">More than one Windows account signed in</h3>\n<p>When two Windows accounts are signed in at the same time, the program runs in whichever account opened it first, and cards print from there. The other account's window shows the same screens. If the first account signs out, the program carries on printing in the other account. Install the card printer for all users, so it's there whichever account is printing (a USB printer usually is).</p>",
  "text": "The program keeps running in the background so cards keep printing. Here's how to open, close and start it. Closing the window Closing the window doesn't stop the program. It keeps running in the notification area by the clock, so cards keep printing. Double-click its icon there, or right-click it and choose Open ID Card Printing Software, to open the window again. Closing the program completely In Settings, under Program, choose Close the program. Choose Close to confirm. Printing stops until the program is opened again from the Start menu. Cards left in the queue print when it's opened. Starting with Windows The setup asks whether to start the program when anyone signs in to Windows. To change your answer, run the setup again and tick or untick Start automatically when anyone signs in to Windows. Where your data is Everything is stored in C:\\ProgramData\\ID Card Printing Software, shared by every Windows account on the PC. Whoever signs in to Windows sees the same templates, clients, people and print queue, and still signs in to the program with their own username and password. Updating keeps it, and uninstalling the program leaves it in place. The database is encrypted so a copy can't be opened on another PC. Anyone who can sign in to Windows on this PC can reach the file, so give Windows accounts on it only to people you trust with the cards' details. Up to version 3.0.11 each Windows account kept its own data. The first time an account runs a newer version, its data moves into the shared folder. If the shared folder already has data of its own, the program asks once which to keep. The other copy stays on the PC as a file. More than one Windows account signed in When two Windows accounts are signed in at the same time, the program runs in whichever account opened it first, and cards print from there. The other account's window shows the same screens. If the first account signs out, the program carries on printing in the other account. Install the card printer for all users, so it's there whichever account is printing (a USB printer usually is)."
 },
 {
  "id": "troubleshooting",
  "title": "Troubleshooting",
  "summary": "Quick fixes for the most common problems.",
  "html": "<p>Quick fixes for the most common problems.</p>\n<h3 id=\"nothing-prints\">Nothing prints</h3>\n<ol><li>Check the printer status in the sidebar, and any banner at the top. See <a href=\"#/help/printer-alerts\">Printer alerts</a>.</li><li>Check the queue isn't paused: choose <b>Resume printing</b> in the <a href=\"#/help/the-print-queue\">print queue</a>.</li><li>In <b>Settings</b>, check the right <b>Card printer</b> is chosen, then choose <b>Print a test card</b>.</li><li>Choose <b>Windows print queue</b> in Settings to see whether Windows is holding the job.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"cards-print-too-light-dull-or-with-a-thin-white-edge\">Cards print too light, dull or with a thin white edge</h3>\n<p>Use <b>Print adjustments</b> in <a href=\"#/help/printer-setup-and-status\">Printer setup and status</a>: more <b>Darkness</b> and <b>Colour</b> for pale cards, and <b>Overprint edges</b> for a white sliver along an edge.</p>\n<h3 id=\"cards-come-out-the-wrong-size-or-cut-off\">Cards come out the wrong size or cut off</h3>\n<p>Check the template's card size matches the cards in the printer, and that the printer driver's own settings (<b>Printing preferences</b>) use the same card size. See <a href=\"#/help/card-templates\">Card templates</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"photos-didnt-match-when-importing\">Photos didn't match when importing</h3>\n<p>Check each photo has the person's first name then last name next to each other (such as <code>John Smith.jpg</code> or <code>John Smith ID Photo.jpg</code>), and that the CSV's names are spelt the same. A row marked <i>More than one photo</i> has two photos with that name: remove the one you don't want. See <a href=\"#/help/importing-people-from-a-spreadsheet\">Importing people from a spreadsheet</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-back-prints-upside-down\">The back prints upside down</h3>\n<p>Turn on <b>Rotate back side 180°</b> in <b>Settings</b>, under <b>Printing</b>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"i-cant-see-card-templates-or-most-of-settings\">I can't see Card templates or most of Settings</h3>\n<p>You're signed in as an operator. Ask an administrator, or see <a href=\"#/help/users-and-passwords\">Users and passwords</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-pc-restarted-in-the-middle-of-printing\">The PC restarted in the middle of printing</h3>\n<p>Nothing is lost. Open the print queue, check the card marked <b>Check output</b>, then choose <b>Resume printing</b>. See <a href=\"#/help/the-print-queue\">The print queue</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-program-says-it-lost-connection\">The program says it lost connection</h3>\n<p>The program's background service stopped, for example because it was closed. Open the program again from the Start menu. The queue is saved and carries on.</p>\n<h3 id=\"windows-says-windows-protected-your-pc\">Windows says <i>Windows protected your PC</i></h3>\n<p>This appears the first time you run the setup, because it isn't code-signed. Choose <b>More info</b>, then <b>Run anyway</b>.</p>",
  "text": "Quick fixes for the most common problems. Nothing prints Check the printer status in the sidebar, and any banner at the top. See Printer alerts. Check the queue isn't paused: choose Resume printing in the print queue. In Settings, check the right Card printer is chosen, then choose Print a test card. Choose Windows print queue in Settings to see whether Windows is holding the job. Cards print too light, dull or with a thin white edge Use Print adjustments in Printer setup and status: more Darkness and Colour for pale cards, and Overprint edges for a white sliver along an edge. Cards come out the wrong size or cut off Check the template's card size matches the cards in the printer, and that the printer driver's own settings (Printing preferences) use the same card size. See Card templates. Photos didn't match when importing Check each photo has the person's first name then last name next to each other (such as John Smith.jpg or John Smith ID Photo.jpg), and that the CSV's names are spelt the same. A row marked More than one photo has two photos with that name: remove the one you don't want. See Importing people from a spreadsheet. The back prints upside down Turn on Rotate back side 180° in Settings, under Printing. I can't see Card templates or most of Settings You're signed in as an operator. Ask an administrator, or see Users and passwords. The PC restarted in the middle of printing Nothing is lost. Open the print queue, check the card marked Check output, then choose Resume printing. See The print queue. The program says it lost connection The program's background service stopped, for example because it was closed. Open the program again from the Start menu. The queue is saved and carries on. Windows says Windows protected your PC This appears the first time you run the setup, because it isn't code-signed. Choose More info, then Run anyway."
 }
];
